An original Apple–1 Computer could sell for between &#36;500,000 and &#36;800,000 when it is <a href="https://www.bonhams.com/press_release/41641/">auctioned off this October</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/hatfield-apple-1.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1062167" /><br/>
There are fewer than 75 Apple–1 Computers known to exist, and a smaller number that are operational. The "Hatfield" Apple–1 is number 48 on the Apple–1 registry, and it is in working condition.<br/>
<br/>
The machine was purchased by Fred Hatfield, a computer enthusiast and employee of Computer Data Systems. It is signed by Steve Wozniak and comes with a letter written to Hatfield and signed by Steve Jobs. Jobs authorized a trade-in for the newly released Apple II after Hatfield complained about the Apple–1 software, but Hatfield declined the offer because he would have needed to pay &#36;400 for the upgrade.<br/>
<br/>
Hatfield sold the Apple–1 in 2013 for &#36;40,000. At the time, it was non-functional, but the buyer fixed it, got it signed by Wozniak, and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2013/05/25/working-apple-1-computer-sells-for-record-auction-price-of-671400/">sold it at auction for &#36;671,400</a>. It's now going up for sale again.<br/>
<br/>
Steve Jobs and Steve Wozniak produced 200 Apple–1 computers in 1976. The device was originally offered as a bare circuit board to be sold as a kit and completed by hobbyists, but Jobs sold 50 fully assembled Apple–1 machines to California-based retailer The Byte Shop. The Byte Shop resold the assembled machines for &#36;666.66.<br/>
<br/>
The Hatfield Apple–1 will be available for public and private viewing at locations in Los Angeles, Boston, San Francisco, and New York, with dates and information <a href="https://www.bonhams.com/press_release/41641/">listed on the auctioneer's website</a>. Bids will be accepted from October 4 to October 14.<br/>
<br/>
